{=============================================================================
  NH3/H2O evaporator temperature glide (Herold et al., Example 9.1)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0362
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Three equilibrium states of an ammonia-water absorption system, as in
  Example 9.1 of Herold, Radermacher and Klein, "Absorption Chillers and
  Heat Pumps", CRC Press, 2nd edition, 2015. The desorber vapour is in
  equilibrium with the entering rich solution (state 1: 150 C, x = 0.3);
  the evaporator receives saturated liquid of the same mass fraction as
  that vapour, at 0 C (state 2); it leaves as a saturated two-phase
  mixture with liquid mass fraction 0.25, all at constant pressure.
  Outputs: vapour mass fractions, saturation pressures, evaporator outlet
  temperature, temperature glide, outlet vapour quality and the minimum
  liquid mass fraction x_e2_min.
  Properties: EES fluid NH3H2O (ammonia mass fraction x, quality Q).
  Inputs : T[1], T[2] [C], x[1], x[3] [kg/kg]
  Outputs: P[i] [Pa], y[i] [kg/kg], T[3] [C], T_glide [C], Q[3], x_e2_min
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa (bar in the original)
  Source : EES example ex9_1.ees (S. A. Klein, F-Chart Software), textbook
           example of Herold et al. (2015) - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"1) Determine the mass fraction of the vapor in equilibrium with the liquid in the desorber - point d"
T[1]=150 [C]	"temperature in desorber"
x[1]=0.3	"mass fraction of ammonia in liquid in desorber [kg/kg]"
P[1]=Pressure(NH3H2O,T=T[1],x=x[1],Q=0)	"pressure in desorber [Pa]"
y[1]=MassFraction(NH3H2O,T=T[1],P=P[1],Q=1)	"mass fraction of ammonia in vapor phase [kg/kg]"

"2)  Assume that saturated liquid of the same mass fraction as the vapor enters the evaporator at 0°C.  What is the mass fraction of the vapor in equilibrium with this liquid? - point e1"
T[2]=0 [C]	"temperature of liquid"
x[2]=y[1]	"mass fraction of the liquid [kg/kg]"
P[2]=Pressure(NH3H2O,T=T[2],x=x[2],Q=0)	"saturation pressure [Pa]"
y[2]=MassFraction(NH3H2O,T=T[2],P=P[2],Q=1)	"mass fraction of ammonia in vapor phase [kg/kg]"

"3)  Assume that the entire liquid is evaporated at constant pressure.  What is the temperature glide? - point e2"
P[3]=P[2]	"evaporated at constant pressure [Pa]"
x[3]=0.25	"mass fraction of liquid outlet from evaporator [kg/kg]"
T[3]=Temperature(NH3H2O,P=P[3],x=x[3],Q=0)	"temperature of the saturated evaporator outlet [C]"
y[3]=MassFraction(NH3H2O,P=P[3],T=T[3],Q=1)	"mass fraction of the vapor in equilibrium with the saturated liquid [kg/kg]"
Q[3]*y[3]+(1-Q[3])*x[3]=x[2]	"mass balance on evaporator gives vapor quality at outlet"
T_glide=T[3]-T[2]	"temperature glide [C]"

"Compute minimum value of x"
T_e2_min=Temperature(NH3H2O,P=P[3],x=x[2],Q=1)	"temperature at P=P[2] and composition y=y[1], as in the original"
x_e2_min=MassFraction(NH3H2O,P=P[3],T=T_e2_min,Q=0)	"saturated liquid pair to y=y[1] at P=P[2] [kg/kg]"
